{"13":[{"k":"p","t":"SECTION 2 BASIC LOGIC FUNCTIONS"},{"k":"p","t":"2.1 HEXADECIMAL NUMBERING SYSTEM"},{"k":"p","t":"Any digital device inherently has two stable states, on or off. Relays are inherently digital, as are switches. An \"on\" device is usually defined as being at a logical one. An \"off\" device is at a logical zero. It follows naturally that the binary numbering system is perfect to describe digital systems, since it has two possible states, 0 and l."},{"k":"p","t":"Basic rules for binary additions follow:"},{"k":"li","t":"(a) 02 (b) 1, (c) ly (4) 12"},{"k":"tr","t":"Plus   +19   +09   +09   +19"},{"k":"tr","t":"Sum   12   1g   1,   109"},{"k":"p","t":"The subscript above indicates that the base of the numbering system is 2. Notice that in example (d), the sum overflowed the base of the numbering system, so a carry was generated into the next most significant digit position. The same thing happens in the decimal numbering system when 1 is added to 9."},{"k":"tr","t":"%40   OR   9910"},{"k":"tr","t":"Plus   +149   + ig"},{"k":"p","t":"1019 10039"},{"k":"p","t":"It is convenient to refer to numbers in the binary system as digits but problems are encountered when the number 9 is exceeded. Therefore, letters were assigned to numbering system:"},{"k":"p","t":"BINARY HEXADECIMAL 0000 Oy 0001 ly 00105 2H 00112 3H 01002 4n 01012 5H 01109 6H 01112 74 10009 8y 10015 9H 10102 Au 1011 By 11009 cy 11019 Du 11109 Eq 11112 Fy"},{"k":"p","t":"2-1"}],"21":[{"k":"p","t":"BLOCK DIAGRAM"},{"k":"p","t":"PROCESSOR BOARD none ASTRO-INVADERS 6-21-60"},{"k":"p","t":"QO cxoce SYSTEM ADDRESS _BUS To"},{"k":"tr","t":"osciu. PP] eux   SI zzz 7   TELE , 7   Z \"ZD> OTHER"},{"k":"tr","t":"leurrers]|   iI   Vf ZI 7 Yh VY Yj 4 BoaRDS"},{"k":"tr","t":"Wie b- =| aaa   Z   v"},{"k":"p","t":"= = wb ENABLE"},{"k":"p","t":"a SCRATCH PAD 280 prom | | PROM PROM PROM PROM PROM PROM RAM"},{"k":"tr","t":"wore   MICRO   ! 2 3 4 5 6 7"},{"k":"p","t":"eonro PROCESSOR"},{"k":"p","t":"rennet dF $08,